00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.840
Chris: Software developers need to use
good coding principles and practices.

00:00:04.449 --> 00:00:10.200
Good coding practices are important in programming
and are achieved by using a variety of principles,

00:00:10.540 --> 00:00:15.779
which may initially seem tedious and time
consuming but may be advantageous long term.

00:00:16.789 --> 00:00:22.590
Leah: Cluttered code can cause problems when it is
further extended, and when the same code needs to be

00:00:22.590 --> 00:00:29.280
worked on by a wider ‘Developer team’, so this is one of
the reasons why there are rules for writing good code.

00:00:30.020 --> 00:00:35.349
Let’s take a look at some of the most common principles
used in programming and software development:

00:00:35.890 --> 00:00:37.199
Chris: The KISS Principle

00:00:37.679 --> 00:00:43.139
The KISS Principle is a term that is widely used
in a variety of different industries so let’s look

00:00:43.139 --> 00:00:45.849
at what it means especially in relation to coding.

00:00:46.519 --> 00:00:54.219
KISS is an acronym for a design principle noted by
the US Navy in 1960 and it stands for ‘Keep it Simple

00:00:54.319 --> 00:01:00.389
and Straightforward’ and covers many different areas
of design including engineering and programming.

00:01:01.079 --> 00:01:06.579
There are other variations of this including ‘keep
it short and simple’ and ‘keep it simple, stupid’.

00:01:07.369 --> 00:01:09.720
Whatever way it is used it means the same thing.

00:01:10.949 --> 00:01:16.509
Leah: ‘Keep it simple stupid’ or even ‘keep it
short simple’ are most commonly used in programming.

00:01:16.910 --> 00:01:22.899
By keeping the programming simple, service and
maintenance are much easier and there are no hard and

00:01:22.899 --> 00:01:30.139
fast rules regarding the implementation of code, only
that it should be simple and therefore easily modified.

00:01:30.989 --> 00:01:34.880
There are a number of ways to keep
your code simple these could include:

00:01:35.419 --> 00:01:41.489
Chris: variables – keep the scope as small as
possible, don’t reuse variables, declare the

00:01:41.489 --> 00:01:44.139
variables you are using and name them properly.

00:01:44.859 --> 00:01:50.599
Functions – keep as small as possible and ‘pure’

00:01:50.599 --> 00:01:53.899
DRY – write ‘Don’t Repeat Yourself’ code

00:01:57.089 --> 00:02:04.569
YAGNI – Use the ‘You Aren’t Gonna Need It’ principle – in
other words write the least amount of code that you need

00:02:05.339 --> 00:02:08.059
SOLID – use the SOLID principle.

00:02:09.539 --> 00:02:13.220
Leah: There are many reasons for
implementing the KISS philosophy.

00:02:13.910 --> 00:02:20.950
It may prevent you from wasting time and effort developing
complicated protocols which take a long time to write.

00:02:21.659 --> 00:02:26.749
However, the primary benefits, are for
those that may use the software afterwards.

00:02:27.209 --> 00:02:32.679
This could include both other programmers and
customers who want a product which is simple to use.

00:02:33.570 --> 00:02:34.789
Chris: SOLID principles

00:02:35.129 --> 00:02:43.419
In programming S O L I D is a mnemonic acronym used for the
five design principles which are intended to make programs

00:02:43.419 --> 00:02:46.729
more ‘understandable’, ‘flexible’ and ‘maintainable’.

00:02:47.329 --> 00:02:48.889
Let’s look at what these are.

00:02:49.979 --> 00:02:53.109
Leah: S - The Single Responsibility Principle

00:02:53.489 --> 00:03:01.039
The Single Responsibility Principle [SRP] states
that every class or method is responsible for one

00:03:01.039 --> 00:03:04.449
single, separate part of a program’s functionality.

00:03:05.179 --> 00:03:08.909
In other words each class should
only have one reason to change.

00:03:09.629 --> 00:03:16.749
Good practice of SRP will prevent side-effects from
future changes and make software easier to implement.

00:03:17.459 --> 00:03:20.139
Chris: O – Open / Closed Principle

00:03:20.819 --> 00:03:25.259
Software entities should be open for
extension but closed for modification.

00:03:25.899 --> 00:03:30.849
In other words you should be able to add new
functionality without changing the existing code.

00:03:31.439 --> 00:03:38.089
So, for example with OCP, if you change one class
there is no need to change all related classes.

00:03:38.749 --> 00:03:42.339
Leah: L – Liskov Substitution Principle

00:03:43.489 --> 00:03:49.349
Properties of one original class should be
replaceable with objects built from classes adapted

00:03:49.359 --> 00:03:52.459
from the original without breaking the application.

00:03:52.889 --> 00:04:01.209
For example, if you had a ‘superclass’ with its own
‘subclasses’, with LSP you can change the objects of the

00:04:01.479 --> 00:04:04.509
‘subclass’ without affecting its associated ‘superclass’.

00:04:05.419 --> 00:04:08.259
Chris: I – Interface Segregation Principle

00:04:08.739 --> 00:04:12.489
No client should be forced to
depend on methods they do not use.

00:04:12.669 --> 00:04:20.259
With ISP it is good practice to have several client specific
interfaces rather than one large general-purpose interface.

00:04:20.899 --> 00:04:26.099
This reduces the side effects that could occur from
using a larger interface as the developers would

00:04:26.109 --> 00:04:29.169
be focused on their own client specific interface.

00:04:30.059 --> 00:04:33.069
Leah: D – Dependency Inversion Principle

00:04:33.609 --> 00:04:38.989
Abstraction should be used to decouple
high-level and low-level modules from each other.

00:04:39.549 --> 00:04:46.299
This way high-level modules which sustain
complex systems, are not affected by low-level

00:04:46.299 --> 00:04:48.869
modules which provide utility features.

00:04:49.609 --> 00:04:55.099
High and low level modules should depend on
abstractions which do not depend on details.

00:04:55.669 --> 00:05:00.169
This means that if the details change,
they do not affect the abstraction.

00:05:00.959 --> 00:05:06.819
Abstraction is one of the key principles of
object-oriented programming where the programmer

00:05:06.829 --> 00:05:13.609
hides all but the relevant data about an object
to reduce its complexity and increase efficiency.

